In math, transpostion is an operation that flips a matrix over its diagonal; that is, transpostion switches the row and column indices of the matrix A to produce another matrix, called the transpose of A and oftern denoted A^T (among other notations)

The transpose of a matrix was introduced in 1858 by The mathematicians Arthur Cayley and Wiliam R. Hamilton who is considered one of the founders of theoretical physics. 
